{
  "dom_id": "step-q52381",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266874/assets/824ac21dd174752d092a31b2b74e267c32b42d1dd1f49b0e3f964def2d7a2886.png)\n\nThe graph of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ (shown above) has discriminant $D.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D=0$\n\nII. $c<0$\n\nIII. $a<0$",
  "worked_solution": "For the given graph of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c:$\n\n- The parabola does not intersect the $x$-axis, so it has no real roots. Therefore, we have $D<0,$ and hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The value $c$ represents the $y$-intercept of the parabola. The $y$-intercept is below the $x$-axis, so it must be negative. Therefore, we must have $c<0$, and hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Finally, notice that $a$ is the leading coefficient. Since the parabola opens downward, we have that $a<0.$ Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
